<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moctezuma II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1466–<a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moctezuma II was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> from 1502 to 1520. He expanded the empire and centralised power, but faced the arrival of Hernan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> in 1519. Taken captive by the Spanish, he died in 1520 during the upheavals that preceded the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="Moctezuma II" width="261" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moctezuma_Mendoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Moctezuma II</b> (r. 1502–1520) tightened central control over tribute and appointments and intervened in the succession at Texcoco, concentrating wealth and power in Tenochtitlan while deepening the resentments that would undermine imperial solidarity when the Spanish arrived.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1504, under <b>Moctezuma II</b>, the fighting grew fiercer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Moctezuma II</b>’s palace, some 200 metres square, held law courts, a war council and a zoo.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Hernán Cortés and his Spanish and Tlaxcalan allies were received in the Aztec capital by <b>Moctezuma II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-8">8 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1501" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1872 1444 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Moctezuma II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2322 1714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1501">What was here in 1501? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Malinche, since then you repose no trust in me, take my son and my two legitimate daughters as hostages; only do not disgrace me, by demanding my person. What will the grandees of my empire say, if they see me taken prisoner?&#8221;</p><footer>To Cortés, on being seized in his palace, 1519 &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; as reported by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who was there &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alas, Malinche! why is it you wish to compel me to bring down total destruction on this town? Our gods are already angry with us, and who can tell what revenge they contemplate against you?&#8221;</p><footer>To Cortés, on his demand to take down the idols of the great temple, 1520 &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; as reported by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who was there &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why does Malinche now turn to me?—to me, who am tired of life, and who could wish never again to hear his name mentioned, for it is he who has plunged me into all this misery!&#8221;</p><footer>On being asked to calm his people, 1520 &#183; tr. John Ingram Lockhart, 1844 &#183; as reported by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who heard it told &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32474/pg32474-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
